Z||000005||johnt671||04-27-2001||09:03 PM||||I have a mercury daily driver that was changed over over before I bought it.  Had the A/C charged and the shop told me that the new stuff just dosen't get as cold as freon.  Works ok though.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spektyr||04-27-2001||09:39 PM||||That's what I've heard as well, the new AC coolant isn't as efficient as freon.<P>My system has no charge at all, it blows air warmer than outside.  My solution is just roll the windows down and drive faster.<P>I may get it charged someday, but I have an engine transplant planned in the somewhat distant future, so I probably won't.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||04-27-2001||09:43 PM||||1. 134 doesnt get as cold as R 12 / thank your EPA.<BR>2. AC normally only drops inside temp about 15-20* below the ambient temp. And thats in system designed for 134. Conversions are never as efficient as orig equip.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodrv6||04-27-2001||10:45 PM||||I converted my 84 to 134 about 3 years ago and it has no problem getting real cold even on a hot July day in Atlanta!  If the system is properly evacuated and recharged, I doubt you would notice much difference between the way the R-12 and the R-134 cool the car.  The system in the Fiero seems to be more than adequate to cool the small interior (relative to other cars).  A common problem is over charging the system.  That can affect performance just as an undercharge can. Also, 134 operates at slightly higher pressures than R-12, so the pressure cycling switch on the accumulator might benefit from an adjustment to raise the pressures at which it cylcles the compressor clutch.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
